From 368ea814c31b173bc6e5d61a720dfe4f47d53a22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:58:39 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Fully unroll requested loops with barriers before the barrier
 passes

The barrier passes turn a loop with a barrier into a b-loop: its body is
split into parallel regions that run for all work-items in every
iteration, the values live across the barriers are saved in context
arrays, and extra barriers isolate the loop control. A request to unroll
such a loop, with '#pragma unroll', opencl_unroll_hint or SPIR-V's Unroll
loop control, is ignored: pocl.barrier is noduplicate, so LLVM's unroller
can't duplicate the body. For the butterflies of sub-group reductions
written with shuffles, or a tree reduction over the local size, the trip
count also only becomes a constant once the stage 1 optimizations folded
the sizes.

Add a pass at the start of stage 2 that fully unrolls innermost loops with
a barrier, an unroll request and a constant trip count, up to LLVM's
default pragma unroll threshold. Every work-item still executes the same
sequence of barriers, so the pass lifts noduplicate from the barrier only
while unrolling these loops. It forms LCSSA for the enclosing loop nest
first, as UnrollLoop only redirects the uses after the loop to the last
iteration through LCSSA phis and expects the parent loops in LCSSA too.
Partial unroll requests are left alone, as they would keep the b-loop.
-cl-opt-disable and optnone disable the pass.
